"""Fresh Version 20 openings, written for LING285 rather than imported from PSYC301."""
NATURAL={
 'oscillation':'**Oscillation comes from a restoring tendency and continued motion working together**. The spring brings a displaced mass inward, but the mass keeps moving far enough to begin the opposite half of the cycle.',
 'directions':'**The direction of movement can change while the reason for oscillation stays the same**. Motion along a spring and motion across a tensioned support both combine a restoring force with inertia.',
 'mass-stiffness':'**Frequency depends on how quickly a system can be pulled back and turned around**. A heavier moving part resists that reversal, while greater stiffness strengthens the pull that produces it.',
 'two-modes':'**Two connected masses have more than one way to move as a system**. They can travel together or oppose one another, and the opposing pattern puts more of the connecting structure to work.',
 'three-modes':'**The three modes are three arrangements of coordinated motion**. Their differences include whether the middle mass travels with the outer masses, stays still, or moves against its neighbors.',
 'many-modes':'**Adding a moving part adds another independent possibility for vibration**. A continuous string extends this idea to material distributed along its length rather than a few separately drawn blocks.',
 'fixed-fixed':'**A string held still at both ends can vibrate only in patterns that respect both supports**. One half-wave fits the lowest mode, and adding further half-waves preserves the same end conditions.',
 'membrane':'**A vibrating surface can organize its movement across two directions at once**. Different regions can move together or oppose one another while several patterns contribute to the overall motion.',
 'scientific-method':'**A physical explanation becomes testable when it predicts a measurement**. Periodic vocal-fold vibration predicts regularly spaced spectral peaks, giving a recording something specific to check.',
 'fixed-free':'**Different end constraints require a different family of wave patterns**. A still endpoint and a freely moving endpoint can be connected by one quarter-wave, then three, then five.',
 'local-change':'**The effect of a change depends on where the changed material participates in a mode**. Moving material makes inertia important, while local deformation makes restoring stiffness important.',
 'air-modes':'**The air travels back and forth along the tract even when its displacement is drawn as a vertical curve**. That curve records the amount of movement at each position rather than a sideways route taken by the particles.',
 'vowel-shape':'**A vowel changes when the tongue and lips reshape the passage containing the air**. The source can keep vibrating while the new shape changes which resonances are favored.',
 'constriction':'**A narrowing changes how the air moves and how strongly it pushes back when compressed**. Its effect on a formant depends on which of those roles dominates at that place in the mode.',
 'u':'**Loading the region near the lips acts where both of the first two air modes move strongly**. The increased inertia slows their cycles, producing the low-F1, low-F2 pattern of the /u/ model.',
 'i':'**The /i/ narrowing meets the first two modes in different local roles**. It loads motion in the first mode but strengthens compression in the second, separating F1 downward from F2 upward.',
 'a':'**Moving the narrowing changes which mode is dominated by compression and which by motion**. In the /a/ comparison, that reversal raises F1 and lowers F2.',
 'source':'**A repeating vocal-fold signal supplies frequencies organized around its repetition rate**. F0 sets the gap between adjacent harmonics, so a faster source spreads the peaks farther apart.',
 'filter':'**The mouth does not have to replace the source to change its sound**. Its resonances favor some of the source harmonics over others, creating the formant pattern associated with the vowel.',
 'sparse-harmonics':'**A resonance is easier to recognize when several nearby harmonics reveal its shape**. At high F0, large gaps between harmonics can leave part of that shape poorly sampled.',
 'spectrogram':'**A changing sound needs a representation that keeps frequency and time together**. The spectrogram preserves their relationship by placing time horizontally, frequency vertically and amplitude in the darkness.',
 'continuous-speech':'**Speaking continuously means the tract is continually changing its configuration**. The formant bands therefore trace transitions through a phrase rather than a sequence of perfectly isolated steady vowels.',
 'splice':'**The information identifying a consonant can continue into its following vowel**. A transition from na can influence the percept even when the consonant fragment itself was taken from ma.',
 'vowel-network':'**Two measured resonances can provide evidence for a vowel decision**. The network receives F1 and F2 and uses learned connections to distribute support across five possible categories.',
 'weights':'**A connection determines how an incoming value counts toward the next neuron**. Its weight scales that contribution, while the signs of both the weight and the input determine its direction.',
 'forward':'**A forward pass uses what the network currently knows to produce a decision**. Each processing stage transforms the values it receives and supplies the next stage with new activations.',
 'probabilities':'**A category decision must separate valid probabilities from a correct interpretation of the input**. Values can be nonnegative and sum to one while still favoring the wrong vowel.',
 'backward':'**An early connection is difficult to judge because its influence travels through later computations**. Backpropagation begins with the simpler downstream effects and reuses them to work out the earlier contributions.',
 'weight-direction':'**A useful correction depends on how a connection contributes and what the output should become**. Raising a weight can help or hurt depending on the incoming sign and the category’s current error.',
 'training-data':'**A training example contains both an observation and its known identity**. The formant row and target row must refer to the same recording so the comparison teaches the intended association.',
 'sequential':'**A network definition specifies how one layer hands its values to the next**. The dimensions must agree at every handoff, including when a new hidden group is inserted before the output.',
 'training-code':'**The training program separates finding a mistake from deciding and applying a correction**. The loss describes the mismatch, the backward pass calculates gradients, and the optimizer changes the parameters.',
 'sigmoid':'**A neuron can transform its weighted sum before passing the result onward**. Sigmoid makes that response bounded and nonlinear, with a strong middle response and flatter behavior near the extremes.',
 'sigmoid-flow':'**Changing a hidden response changes the evidence available to every later stage**. Once sigmoid transforms an activation, the downstream values have to be computed from the transformed result.',
 'overlap':'**Measured vowels do not always fall into perfectly separate patches of formant space**. Similar F1 and F2 values can belong to different labeled tokens, making classification less than a simple lookup.',
 'accuracy':'**Accuracy asks how often the selected category matches the target**. It can improve over guessing while leaving many individual recordings incorrectly classified.',
 'initialization':'**The first weights shape the route taken by learning before useful distinctions emerge**. Different starts can therefore produce different curves even when the displayed final accuracies agree.',
 'recurrent':'**A sequence needs a way for information from one step to matter at the next**. Recurrent state provides such a connection, allowing current processing to depend on prior context.',
 'next-token':'**Prediction can be organized around the same question across different kinds of data**. Once the sequence is represented as vectors, the model uses the available context to assign support to possible continuations.',
 'phonotactics':'**A language restricts which sound sequences fit particular syllable positions**. A combination allowed across a syllable break can still be excluded from the beginning of a single syllable.',
 'letter-guess':'**A short word beginning already carries information about what can follow**. Learning its likely completions requires capturing those restrictions rather than choosing every next letter equally.',
 'heads':'**Several relationships can be processed in parallel before a representation moves to the next layer**. Attention heads contribute alongside one another, while depth supplies successive transformations of their combined information.',
 'dynamic-depth':'**A state can be understood by following the rule that transforms it**. Physical time steps and network layers both provide a sequence of transformations, even though their rules and materials differ.',
 'agents':'**A larger system can organize several models around connected jobs**. A draft, a critique and a revision become parts of a feedback process before a final response is returned.',
 'economy':'**A changing economic quantity can be modeled through the relationships acting on it**. Feedback can stabilize a deviation or amplify it, giving the system a pattern that can be compared with other dynamical models.',
 'speech':'**Speech depends on many movements being coordinated without direct sight of most of them**. Children develop much of that control before the explicit instruction normally used to teach reading and writing.',
 'acoustics':'**The shape of the tract becomes audible through the air inside it**. Acoustic phonetics studies that resulting sound and connects its pattern with the articulation that produced it.',
 'ai-science':'**An impressive response is a behavior to explain rather than a complete explanation of its own mechanism**. Examining the computation helps connect the observed response with evidence about how it was produced.',
 'reasoning':'**An idea is understood when its relationships can be used beyond a memorized phrase**. Comparing cases helps identify what remains causally important when the surface appearance changes.',
 'fricatives':'**A narrow passage can preserve noisy airflow while vocal-fold vibration changes**. That is why /f/ and /v/ can share their place and manner while differing in voicing.',
 'stops':'**Closing the mouth does not settle whether air can still escape through the nose**. The velum controls the nasal route, distinguishing a nasal stop from an otherwise similar oral stop.',
 'consonants':'**A consonant comparison requires several physical questions to be kept separate**. Voicing, the place of narrowing and the manner of airflow can agree or differ independently across a pair.',
 'ai-skills':'**Using learned information and acquiring new information are different abilities**. Recognition can operate with existing weights, while learning changes the parameters that later recognition uses.',
 'closure':'**Incomplete information can still support a coherent perceived whole**. A triangle with missing edges and a sequence with a missing word both require relationships among the supplied parts.',
 'completion':'**A missing part is constrained by the context around it**. Choosing a suitable continuation therefore provides evidence that the system has learned useful relationships within the sequence.',
 'learning':'**An initial guess can be produced before the network has useful knowledge**. Feedback about its errors supplies the information needed to improve the connections behind later guesses.',
 'sgd':'**The averaging example learns by repeatedly moving partway toward an observation**. The sampled value supplies a direction, while the learning rate sets how strongly that observation changes the estimate.',
 'initial-estimate':'**The first estimate gives learning a place to begin rather than an answer already justified by the data**. Starting above or below the target changes the path without changing what the observations support.',
 'sgd-down':'**An observation lower than the current estimate calls for a downward correction**. Subtracting part of the positive discrepancy moves the estimate toward that observation rather than replacing it outright.',
 'sgd-up':'**An observation higher than the estimate calls for an upward correction**. The discrepancy is negative, so subtracting a fraction of it increases the estimate in the useful direction.',
 'rate':'**The size of each correction affects both how fast the estimate moves and how strongly it follows sample variation**. Larger steps react quickly, while smaller steps make the path steadier.',
 'system':'**The behavior of a system depends on relationships among its changing parts**. Knowing which elements are present is only the beginning of explaining how they produce an organized result.',
 'octopus':'**A complex motor action depends on coordination rather than isolated muscle movements**. The octopus organizes its arms in relation to its environment, just as speaking organizes several articulators around a communicative action.',
 'greedy':'**The next cheap move can be part of an expensive full route**. A greedy choice is fast because it considers immediate cost while leaving the remaining path out of that comparison.',
 'exhaustive':'**Trying every route can establish the best answer in the finite path problem**. Its cost comes from evaluating many candidates, including repeated pieces shared by different routes.',
 'dynamic-programming':'**A shared remainder of a problem can be solved once instead of repeatedly**. Dynamic programming keeps that answer available for the larger alternatives that depend on it.',
 'value':'**A stored value includes the best continuation from a state to the goal**. Adding it to an immediate edge cost makes the comparison about the whole remaining route rather than its first step.',
 'least-time':'**The shortest distance does not have to produce the shortest travel time**. When speed differs between regions, the useful route comparison includes both the path length and the rate of travel.',
 'settling':'**A return rule can oppose the present gap from a target**. As that gap shrinks, the correction shrinks too, producing settling in the first-order example rather than repeated inertial overshoot.',
 'position-velocity':'**Being at the middle and being stopped are different states**. A mass can pass equilibrium with velocity or reach a turning point away from equilibrium with no velocity at that instant.',
 'frequency':'**The speed of repetition differs from the size of a movement**. Frequency counts cycles in time, while amplitude records the extent of the oscillation.',
 'cochlea':'**The inner ear uses different mechanical properties to separate a mixture of frequencies**. Light, stiff regions favor faster vibration, while heavier, more compliant regions favor slower vibration.',
 'euler':'**A computer can approximate continuous change by updating a state in small steps**. The values used in each update matter because changing their order can change the simulated behavior.',
 'templates':'**Recognizing a new pattern can depend on how it matches information already stored**. Numerical input vectors and template weights provide a way to calculate that relationship.',
 'hierarchy':'**A recognition decision can emerge through several changing representations**. Each stage supplies the next with processed information rather than passing along only the untouched original signal.',
 'brain-model':'**A mathematical neuron keeps selected relationships from biological processing while simplifying the physical details**. Incoming activity is combined through connection strengths to produce a calculated response.',
 'bad-data':'**Learning follows the observations it receives rather than automatically knowing which ones are mistakes**. A corrupted value can therefore produce a well-executed update toward the wrong target.',
 'air-pressure':'**Sound can travel while individual air particles move only locally**. Their repeated crowding and separation creates a pressure pattern that passes through the medium.',
 'waveform':'**A time trace follows one measurement as the signal changes**. Its repeated pattern can describe vibration at one position without being a map of the whole vibrating medium.',
 'mixture':'**One recorded sound can combine several simpler contributions**. Its waveform reflects their sum, while its spectrum separates the frequencies and their relative strengths.',
 'inertia':'**A moving object carries motion through a position unless a force changes that motion**. Inertia therefore explains why reaching the spring’s natural length does not immediately end an oscillation.',
 'differential':'**A rule for the rate of change links the present state with what happens next**. Following that relationship produces a predicted path rather than a disconnected list of states.',
 'recognition':'**A measured signal and a decision about its identity are different representations**. Learned weights determine how the numerical observations become support for the candidate categories.',
 'network-shape':'**The network’s architecture specifies how many values are received and produced at each processing stage**. In the vowel example, two inputs pass through three hidden groups before reaching five output categories.',
 'levels':'**More complex organization can be built around the same basic numerical operations**. Neurons contribute to networks, sequence models use context, and agentic systems connect models through tasks and messages.',
 'atomic-analogy':'**A wave-pattern comparison can connect systems without making their physical parts identical**. Organized modes are the useful relationship, while a familiar shell picture is not a literal account of every atomic process.',
 'software':'**A computational task can be organized through explicit instructions, learned parameters or communication among models**. The three software categories separate those roles while allowing them to be combined in one working system.',
 'learned-knowledge':'**The input supplies a current case, while the learned weights supply relationships used to interpret it**. Changing the case and learning from an error are therefore different ways to change a response.',
 'connection-scale':'**A useful response can depend on information distributed over many connections**. The comparison between model weights and brain connections concerns that organization rather than a fact that must be stored in only one place.',
 'two-vibrators':'**The voiced source and the air passage are two connected vibrating systems**. The vocal folds supply the driving motion, while the tract’s shape changes how its air responds.'
}
